🌳 DOM in JavaScript — Simple Explanation

DOM = Document Object Model

If you're learning JavaScript for Playwright, DOM is very important because Playwright interacts with the elements on a web page, and those elements are represented through the DOM.

1. Think of a webpage like a tree

Suppose your HTML is:

<html>
  <body>
    <h1>Login Page</h1>
    <input id="username">
    <input id="password">
    <button>Login</button>
  </body>
</html>

The browser converts this HTML into a DOM tree:

Document
   │
   └── html
        │
        └── body
             ├── h1
             │    └── Login Page
             │
             ├── input
             │    └── username
             │
             ├── input
             │    └── password
             │
             └── button
                  └── Login

JavaScript can use this DOM to find, change, add, or remove elements.

2. Why do we need DOM?

For example, this HTML:

<h1 id="title">Welcome</h1>

JavaScript can find it:

let heading = document.getElementById("title");

Now we can change it:

heading.textContent = "Hello Geeta";

The webpage changes from:

Welcome

to:

Hello Geeta
3. Common DOM methods
getElementById()

Find an element using its ID:

let username = document.getElementById("username");
querySelector()

Find the first matching element:

let button = document.querySelector("button");

or:

let username = document.querySelector("#username");
querySelectorAll()

Find multiple matching elements:

let buttons = document.querySelectorAll("button");
4. Changing HTML
document.getElementById("title").textContent = "Login Page";

Changing the HTML inside an element:

document.getElementById("message").innerHTML = "<b>Hello</b>";
5. Changing CSS

HTML:

<h1 id="title">Hello</h1>

JavaScript:

let title = document.getElementById("title");

title.style.color = "red";
title.style.fontSize = "30px";
6. Handling a button click

HTML:

<button id="login">Login</button>

JavaScript:

let button = document.getElementById("login");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    console.log("Login button clicked");
});

This is called an event.

Common events:

click
input
change
mouseover
keydown
submit
⭐ DOM vs Playwright

This is especially important for you.

JavaScript DOM

You might write:

document.getElementById("username").value = "Geeta";

You're directly interacting with the browser's DOM.

Playwright

You normally write:

await page.locator("#username").fill("Geeta");

or:

await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Login" }).click();

So you can think of it as:

HTML
  ↓
Browser
  ↓
DOM
  ↓
JavaScript can interact with DOM
  ↓
Playwright can locate/interact with page elements